W: 2. Quickly he saluted me,
W: Meaning to shew his breeding;
W: I bowed to him right gracefully,
W: His courtesy exceeding.
W: He ask'd me where I went so soon
W: And longed to begin a parley.
W: I told him to the next market town
W: A-purpose to sell my barley.
W:
W: 3. "In this purse, sweet girl," says he,
W: "Twenty pounds lie fairly
W: Seek no further one to buy
W: For I'll take all your barley.
W: Twenty more would buy delight
W: Your body I love so dearly,
W: If you would lay with me all night
W: And go home in the morning early."
W:
W: 4. "If twenty pounds could buy the globe
W: It's this I never do, sir.
W: Or were my kin as poor as Job
W: I wo'd not raise 'em so, sir.
W: If I lay with you this night
W: We'd get a young child together
W: And you'd be gone ere the nine months end
W: And where should I find a father?"
W:
W: 5. He told me he had married been
W: Fourteen years and longer,
W: Or else he'd take me for his own
W: And tie the knot much stronger.
W: I bade him then no further roam
W: But manage his wedlock fairly;
W: And keep his gold for his wife at home
W: And some other shall have my barley.
W:
